    "id": "preconception-checkup",
    "title": "Before Trying to Conceive: A Sexual-Health and Preconception Checkup",
    "category": "Education",
    "art": "rose",
    "excerpt": "What deserves a conversation before we start trying?",
    "body": "<p>Planning a pregnancy can begin with a conversation, not a shopping cart full of supplements. A preconception visit is a chance to discuss health, medicines, infection prevention and reproductive goals before decisions become urgent.</p>\n<p>It can also be useful when pregnancy is a possibility rather than an immediate plan. You do not need to promise a timetable, a particular family structure or certainty about parenthood to ask good questions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"put-your-actual-goals-on-the-agenda\">Put your actual goals on the agenda</h2>\n<p>Explain whether you are considering pregnancy soon, exploring future options or trying to understand how a health condition affects the decision. Include the practical conditions that matter: care access, support, finances, work demands, disability accommodations or uncertainty about the plan.</p>\n<p>An original starting sentence is: “I would like to understand what deserves attention before a pregnancy and what can wait.” That invites priorities instead of an unmanageable list of everything that could possibly go wrong.</p>\n<p>If another person is involved in the reproductive plan, discuss how information and responsibilities will be shared without making one person responsible for everyone's health behavior.</p>\n<h2 id=\"bring-the-complete-medication-and-supplement-list\">Bring the complete medication and supplement list</h2>\n<p>CDC recommends discussing medical conditions and all medicines, including prescriptions, over-the-counter products, vitamins and supplements, before pregnancy. The exact product and dose help the clinician assess safety and timing. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/pregnancy/about/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 1: CDC\">[1]</a></p>\n<p>Do not stop a medicine independently because a pregnancy is being considered. Some treatments need adjustment, while leaving a condition untreated may also create risk. Ask what changes, if any, are needed, who will coordinate them and when to review the plan.</p>\n<p>Include nonprescription products marketed for hormones, fertility, relaxation or sexual performance. “Natural” does not tell the care team what the ingredients do or whether they suit a possible pregnancy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"review-prevention-before-it-becomes-an-urgent-question\">Review prevention before it becomes an urgent question</h2>\n<p>Vaccination history, infection screening and sexual-health concerns can be part of the visit. Bring available records and describe relevant activities rather than assuming an old “full panel” covered everything. A clinician can identify which tests or vaccines are appropriate and whether timing affects pregnancy planning. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/pregnancy/about/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 1: CDC\">[1]</a></p>\n<p>Some people also need discussion of HIV prevention, previous STIs, reproductive procedures or a partner's health history. These are practical care questions, not tests of whether someone is sufficiently responsible to become a parent.</p>\n<p>Agree on how new results will be communicated and what follow-up is necessary before or during a pregnancy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ask-about-folic-acid-and-everyday-support\">Ask about folic acid and everyday support</h2>\n<p>CDC recommends 400 micrograms of folic acid daily before and during early pregnancy to help prevent certain brain and spine birth defects. Some medical histories require individualized advice, so a larger dose should not be assumed to be better. Ask how the recommendation fits your situation and existing products. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/pregnancy/about/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 1: CDC\">[1]</a></p>\n<p>Discuss nutrition, alcohol, tobacco and other substances without trying to design a perfect lifestyle first. A useful conversation identifies realistic support and the most important next step. Financial or energy constraints deserve a plan that compensates for them, rather than instructions that assume unlimited time and money.</p>\n<h2 id=\"include-family-history-and-fertility-questions\">Include family history and fertility questions</h2>\n<p>Tell the clinician about relevant inherited conditions, previous pregnancy losses, reproductive concerns or treatment that may affect fertility. They can explain whether genetic counseling or specialist assessment would be useful. A referral is a way to answer a particular question, not a prediction that something will go wrong. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/pregnancy/about/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 1: CDC\">[1]</a></p>\n<p>If conception requires donor sperm, donor eggs or another supported pathway, ask about planning and assessment early. You do not need to follow a calendar intended for a different reproductive situation. ASRM's fertility guidance recognizes that evaluation should fit the circumstances involved. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.asrm.org/practice-guidance/practice-committee-documents/fertility-evaluation-of-infertile-women-a-committee-opinion-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 2: ASRM (2026)\">[2]</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"leave-with-a-short-priority-list\">Leave with a short priority list</h2>\n<p>This original Vulpes Ignis appointment sheet has four sections: decisions needed before trying; tasks that can happen alongside planning; people responsible for follow-up; and the date to review unanswered questions.</p>\n<p>Include one relationship question if useful: “How do we keep affection and sexual choice from becoming only a conception project?” Planning for a pregnancy can support health without turning either person into a system that must perform on command.</p>",
